<title>USA: U.S. Aug producer prices rose 0.3 pct.</title>
<headline>U.S. Aug producer prices rose 0.3 pct.</headline>
<dateline>WASHINGTON 1996-09-12</dateline>
<text>
<p>U.S. Labor Department monthly</p>
<p>producer price index (1982 equals 100):</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> -				 Aug   July   Aug 96/95</p>
<p> Finished Goods		0.3   Unch     3.0</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	 -0.1    0.1     1.4</p>
<p> -Consumer Foods	     1.0    0.2     5.1</p>
<p> -Energy Goods		 0.7   -0.9     6.4</p>
<p> Finished Goods Index-X  131.9  131.5     N/A</p>
<p>X-Data not seasonally adjusted. N/A-Not Available.  </p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Finished Goods		Aug   July   Aug 96/95</p>
<p> -Gasoline		     0.9   -2.8    13.5</p>
<p> -Heating Oil		  4.1    4.4    21.7</p>
<p> -Tobacco Products	  -0.2    0.1     3.0</p>
<p> -Passenger Cars	    -0.2   -0.9     1.8</p>
<p> -Capital Equipment	 -0.1    0.3     1.0</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  0.2   -0.3    Unch</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy	  0.1   -0.3    -1.8</p>
<p> -Manufact Materials	Unch   -0.5    -2.3</p>
<p> -Construction Materials   0.3   -0.1     0.8</p>
<p>    Percent Changes:   Seasonally Adj.  Unadjusted</p>
<p> Intermediate Goods	  Aug   July   Aug 96/95</p>
<p> -Intermed.Energy Goods    0.9   -0.6     6.4</p>
<p> Crude Goods		   0.2    2.0-R  13.9</p>
<p> -Less Food, Energy-X	0.1   -1.6   -12.4</p>
<p> -Food/Feedstuffs	   -0.3    2.7    23.5</p>
<p> -Nonfood Materials	  0.4    1.7-R   7.2</p>
<p> -Energy Materials-Y	 0.7    3.9-R  23.5</p>
<p> -Petroleum		    3.2    3.5    25.5</p>
<p>X-Excludes crude petroleum. Y-Includes crude petroleum. Note - Tobacco products and petroleum are not seasonally adjusted.  </p>
<p>Wall Street economists had forecast August producer prices to rise 0.1 percent. Excluding food and energy, they also expected a 0.1 percent increase.</p>
<p>Because of technical adjustments to the natural gas component of the crude PPI, the department revised the overall crude index and several of its components for July.</p>
<p>Previously, the department reported July crude goods as rising 1.2 percent, crude nonfood materials increasing 0.1 percent and crude energy materials up 1.4 percent.</p>
